# The HELP view for {{TOOL}} — what you type at the shell.
#
# Hand this file to an agent with the tool's docs and say "fill this in".
# Everything below the header is `command | description`, grouped by `= HEADING`.
#
# Two rules make a good file:
#   Describe the config in THIS repo, not the upstream manual.
#   Write the description as WHY, not what. "see what you are about to push"
#   beats "show the diff".

name:    {{TOOL}}
what:    one line — what this tool is for
docs:    https://
install: how you would get it on a new machine
check:   {{TOOL}}
probe:   help

# The `help` probe runs `{{TOOL}} --help` and reads BOTH halves of it: the flags
# and the subcommands. A flag-only read reports git as four rows.
#
# Two knobs, for the tools that do it differently:
#   help_cmd:     {{TOOL}} help {}      when --help is not how you ask
#   subcommands:  no                    when the tool has none, so nothing walks
# `{}` is where the subcommand goes.

= EVERYDAY

# A literal row. The command is what you type, and no probe touches it.
{{TOOL}} --version   | check it is really installed

# A probed row. `@` marks it as a node the probe resolves; for `help` that is a
# subcommand or a flag, and opening it is what fetches its children.
@{{TOOL}} status     | what state you are actually in

= NESTING

# One --help call costs about 90 ms, so the tree is walked ONE NODE AT A TIME,
# when you open it — never eagerly. (Measured: git has 178 subcommands, so a
# full walk would take ~16 s and freeze the pane.)
#
# Indent two spaces per level. `enter` or `l` opens a node, `h` closes it.
# Anything you write by hand stays; opening the node appends what the probe
# found under it.
@{{TOOL}} run        | the one you use daily
  --dry-run          | show what it would do, change nothing
  --verbose          | when the quiet version lied to you

# Searching does not hide a match behind a closed parent — filtering opens
# every ancestor of a hit, then drops the headings left with no children.

= ALL FLAGS (from --help)

# Leave this group last and empty. The probe fills it in, and putting it at the
# bottom is what keeps a tool with 149 flags from being the first thing you see.

# A literal `|` inside a row is `\|` — half the useful rows are pipelines.
